1. Where the gun is, per attitude (kit proxy)

pose                                           COM r (mm) tip r tip over rim beam in-section / along-tangent tilt (deg from vertical)  grip base r, height over rim, exit direction
A  opening pose, barrel tangent (fed wire)           118     55.3       5.0    32.5 / 37.0                          r 234, 133, dir (-0.22, -0.84, 0.50)
B  radial plane, over the bore (no wire)              50     53.8       7.5    30.0 / 0.0                           r 167, 154, dir (-0.82, 0.00, 0.57)
B2 radial plane, grip up, over the axis               14     53.8       7.5    30.0 / 0.0                           r 38, 272, dir (-0.09, 0.00, 1.00)

   The crown of scenes/datum-03-rim-crown balances the gun about its ball ring (radius 66 mm): A has the centre of mass 118 mm out, which is why
   round 3 there needs a counterweight about as heavy as the gun. B has it about 50 mm out, inside the ring.

2. With the pose-A roll and hole dials held, the vertical (yaw) dial changes the beam angle in the radial-vertical section:
   vertical dial  -45 deg : in-section tilt  42.9 deg, along-tangent tilt  18.5 deg
   vertical dial  -30 deg : in-section tilt  39.0 deg, along-tangent tilt  29.4 deg
   vertical dial  -15 deg : in-section tilt  32.5 deg, along-tangent tilt  37.0 deg
   vertical dial    0 deg : in-section tilt  22.8 deg, along-tangent tilt  41.8 deg
   vertical dial   15 deg : in-section tilt   9.9 deg, along-tangent tilt  44.2 deg
   vertical dial   30 deg : in-section tilt  -4.7 deg, along-tangent tilt  44.5 deg
   The in-section tilt sets how much of the beam goes to the wall side and how much to the plate side. What removing the wire frees is the
   rotation about the BEAM axis (below), not yaw about the vertical.

3. Rotation about the beam axis through the dot (turns the gun about its own optical axis; the round beam does not change)

   A  opening pose, barrel tangent (fed wire)    grip base moves 2.06 mm per degree of spin (lever 118 mm); fibre twist per degree of spin 0.91 deg
   B  radial plane, over the bore (no wire)      grip base moves 2.06 mm per degree of spin (lever 118 mm); fibre twist per degree of spin 0.91 deg
   B2 radial plane, grip up, over the axis       grip base moves 2.06 mm per degree of spin (lever 118 mm); fibre twist per degree of spin 0.91 deg
   The wobble sweep (2 mm at 80 Hz in the recorded settings) runs along the gun's local x; spin turns it away from across-the-seam: width across the seam x cos(spin):
   spin 5 deg -> 99.6 % of the swing width across the seam (and 0.17 mm of a 2 mm swing along it)
   spin 10 deg -> 98.5 % of the swing width across the seam (and 0.35 mm of a 2 mm swing along it)
   spin 20 deg -> 94.0 % of the swing width across the seam (and 0.68 mm of a 2 mm swing along it)
   spin 30 deg -> 86.6 % of the swing width across the seam (and 1.00 mm of a 2 mm swing along it)
   With a fed wire the wire bracket fixes the spin; with none, the wobble direction and the fibre are what limit it. Both numbers are the proxy's.

4. Cable laid flat behind an orbiting gun (datum-03b geometry, G1 route, plan view only)
   A  opening pose, barrel tangent (fed wire): exit at r = 234 mm, plan heading -15 deg from radially outward (+ = counterclockwise)
     trailing side clockwise : turn 142 deg (arc 868 mm), then a circle of radius 718 mm.  380 deg: 5.63 m OVER 5 m   190 deg: 3.25 m
     trailing side counter-clockwise: turn 151 deg (arc 923 mm), then a circle of radius 818 mm.  380 deg: 6.35 m OVER 5 m   190 deg: 3.64 m
   B  radial plane, over the bore (no wire): exit at r = 167 mm, plan heading 0 deg from radially outward (+ = counterclockwise)
     trailing side clockwise : turn 155 deg (arc 944 mm), then a circle of radius 738 mm.  380 deg: 5.84 m OVER 5 m   190 deg: 3.39 m
     trailing side counter-clockwise: turn 155 deg (arc 944 mm), then a circle of radius 738 mm.  380 deg: 5.84 m OVER 5 m   190 deg: 3.39 m
   So the exit direction in B is not the fix: both A and B leave the grip base radially outward, need a turn of 140 to 160 degrees before the fibre can
   run round the tube, and need about 5.8 to 6 m for a continuous 380 degrees. What the missing wire buys is a bidirectional weld (each half 190 degrees
   from the first tack, cable unwound between): about 3.4 m, inside the 5 m, with every bend at or above 350 mm by construction.
   (datum-03b's scene, pose A at a fixed 700 mm track: 5.6 m and a 262 mm bend, which is consistent with the above: the track radius that satisfies the 350 mm bend is about 740 to 800 mm.)

5. The nozzle tip in B sits 7.5 mm above the rim plane and 53.8 mm from the axis (bore wall at 61.85): inside the bore, as in A.
   The barrel then runs up and toward the axis, so nothing of it crosses the rim. For a room-fixed gun (not a crown, where the gun rides with the tube) the tube can then
   slide out either way along y, the tangent at the station, because the barrel lies in the x-z plane and the tip is 7.5 mm above the rim; in A the barrel lies along -y, so only the
   slide away from it (+y) is free. (calc/05 and calc/07 hold for A: the horizontal shuttle clears while 16 cos(beta) > 6.35 mm, with the wire retracted; a straight lift-out needs about 144 mm.)
